          "chunk_index": 1,
          "chunk_id": "s01_c01",
          "anchor_id": "cue_s01_c1_equilibrium_propagation_promising_en",
          "text": "Equilibrium propagation is a promising energy-based alternative to backpropagation for training neural networks on brains or neuromorphic hardware, but it classically demands two things physical substrates cannot easily deliver: perfectly symmetric weights and infinitesimally small nudges.",
          "expected_role": "method",
          "cue_keywords": [
            "equilibrium",
            "propagation",
            "promising",
            "energy-based",
            "alternative",
            "backpropagation",
            "neural",
            "networks",
            "brains",
            "neuromorphic"
          ],
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "chunk_index": 2,
          "chunk_id": "s01_c02",
          "anchor_id": "cue_s01_c2_iclr_2024_friedrich_miescher_institu",
          "text": "This ICLR 2024 paper from the Friedrich Miescher Institute and the University of Basel, by Axel Laborieux and Friedemann Zenke, cleanly separates the two resulting sources of gradient bias and shows how to remove them.",
          "expected_role": "result",
          "cue_keywords": [
            "iclr",
            "2024",
            "friedrich",
            "miescher",
            "institute",
            "university",
            "basel",
            "axel",
            "laborieux",
            "friedemann"
          ],
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "chunk_index": 3,
          "chunk_id": "s01_c03",
          "anchor_id": "cue_s01_c3_finite_nudge_bias_eliminated_exactly",
          "text": "Finite-nudge bias is eliminated exactly with a Cauchy integral, while weight-asymmetry bias is tamed by a new homeostatic loss that penalizes asymmetry of the Jacobian rather than the weights.",
          "expected_role": "figure",
          "cue_keywords": [
            "finite-nudge",
            "bias",
            "eliminated",
            "exactly",
            "cauchy",
            "integral",
            "while",
            "weight-asymmetry",
            "bias",
            "tamed"
